i\f0 Extension\i0\f2 * \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Extension}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Extension}\f0 Extension = \f2 '.' IDENTIFIER \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Lifetime}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Lifetime}\f0 Lifetime = \i Call\i0\f2  | \i\f0 Local\i0\f2  | \i\f0 Reference\i0\f2  | \i\f0 Thrown\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Call}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Call}\i0 Call = \f2 '#' \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Local}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Local}\f0 Local = \f2 '$' \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Reference}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Reference}\f0 Reference = \f2 '^' IDENTIFIER \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 {\*\bkmkstart ID_prod_46_Thrown}{\*\bkmkend ID_prod_46_Thrown}\f0 Thrown = \f2 '!' \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_definitions}{\*\bkmkend ID_definitions}\b\fs35\f1 6 Definitions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_def_45_overview}{\*\bkmkend ID_def_45_overview}\fs31 6.1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Definitions declare new types that can be used throughout a program. Types can have properties, methods, and nested definitions.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_def_45_functions_45_operators}{\*\bkmkend ID_def_45_functions_45_operators}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 6.2 Functions, Procedures, and Operators\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A routine is introduced by one of three keywords, and that keyword \'97 not the spelling of the name \'97 decides how the routine behaves:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 function\i0  declares a pure function: its inputs are borrowed and immutable, and it returns a result without side effects. A function is applied prefix, by writing its name before a parenthesized argument list.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 procedure\i0  declares a routine that may mutate its inputs and have side effects (builders, I/O).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 operator\i0  declares an operator: a pure routine applied infix between its two operands (or prefix, for a unary operator).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 The three identifier forms from the lexical chapter \'97 alphanumeric, operator-character, and single-quoted string identifiers \'97 are interchangeable as names here. A function may be named with operator characters, and an operator may be named with letters or a quoted string; what makes one a function and the other an operator is the declaration keyword, never the first character of the name.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function 'add seven'(n: int) returns int      ; string-named function\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    n + 7\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function ++(n: int) returns int               ; operator-char-named function\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    n + 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let a 'add seven'(35)                          ; applied prefix -> 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let b ++(41)                                   ; applied prefix -> 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Operators may be declared at the top level, not only as members of a type. Like functions, they accept any of the three name forms:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
operator <+>(left: int, right: int) returns int    ; operator-char name\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    left + right\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
operator 'divided by'(left: int, right: int) returns int   ; quoted name\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    left / right\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let c 40 <+> 2              ; infix -> 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let d 84 'divided by' 2     ; infix -> 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Built-in arithmetic, comparison, and logical operators carry their usual precedences. A custom operator with a text or single-quoted name binds most weakly of all \'97 at the same level as \f2 ||\f0  \'97 so the built-in arithmetic in an expression combines before it. For example, given an operator \f2 'minus'\f0  that subtracts its right operand from its left, \f2 20 'minus' 3 + 5\f0  parses as \f2 20 'minus' (3 + 5)\f0  and yields \f2 12\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_category_45_DEFINITIONS}{\*\bkmkend ID_category_45_DEFINITIONS}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 6.3 Definitions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Definitions declare new types that can be used throughout a program.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_STRUCTURE_45_DEFINITIONS}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_STRUCTURE_45_DEFINITIONS}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 6.3.1 Structure Definitions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The define keyword creates a new structure type with named properties.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Point(x: int, y: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Point(3, 4)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 \{"_type":"Point","x":3,"y":4\} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Point(x: int, y: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Point(3, 4).x\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 3 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Point(x: int, y: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p Point(3, 4)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
p.x + p.y\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 7 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Point(x: int, y: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Rectangle(origin: Point, width: int, height: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Rectangle(Point(0, 0), 10, 5).width\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 10 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_UNION_45_DEFINITIONS}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_UNION_45_DEFINITIONS}\b\fs28\f1 6.3.2 Union Definitions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Unions define types with multiple variants. Each variant can optionally carry a value.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Option union(Some: int, None)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Some(5)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 \{"_type":"Option","_variant":"Some","value":5\} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Option union(Some: int, None)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
None\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 \{"_type":"Option","_variant":"None"\} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union(Ok: int, Err: String)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Ok(42)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 \{"_type":"Result","_variant":"Ok","value":42\} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union(Ok: int, Err: String)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Err("error")\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 \{"_type":"Result","_variant":"Err","value":"error"\} \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Option union(Some: int, None)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Option.Some(5).value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 5 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_FUNCTION_45_NAMES}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_FUNCTION_45_NAMES}\b\fs28\f1 6.3.3 Function Names\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A routine's role is set by its declaration keyword, not by how its name is spelled. A function may be named with ordinary letters, with a single-quoted string identifier (which may contain spaces), or with operator characters; in every case it is an ordinary function, applied by writing its name before a parenthesized argument.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 function 'add seven'(n: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    n + 7\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
'add seven'(35)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 function ++(n: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    n + 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
++(41)\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_OPERATOR_45_DEFINITIONS}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_OPERATOR_45_DEFINITIONS}\b\fs28\f1 6.3.4 Operator Definitions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The operator keyword declares an operator, applied infix between its two operands. Like functions, operators may be named with operator characters or with a text or single-quoted name. Operators may be declared at the top level, not only inside a type. A text-named operator binds more weakly than the built-in arithmetic operators, so the arithmetic combines first.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 operator <+>(left: int, right: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    left + right\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
40 <+> 2\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 operator 'plus'(left: int, right: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    left + right\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
40 'plus' 2\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 operator 'divided by'(left: int, right: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    left / right\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
84 'divided by' 2\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 operator 'minus'(left: int, right: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
