choose Option[int].None: when v: Some: v else -1\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 -1 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_category_45_VARIANT_45_CONSTRUCTION}{\*\bkmkend ID_category_45_VARIANT_45_CONSTRUCTION}\b\fs31\f1 7.12 Variant Construction\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Union variants can be constructed explicitly using Type.Variant syntax.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_VARIANT_45_CONSTRUCT}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_VARIANT_45_CONSTRUCT}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 7.12.1 Construct Variants\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Variants are constructed with Type.Variant(value) syntax.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union: (Ok: int, Error: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose Result.Ok(42): when x: Ok: x else 0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 42 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union: (Ok: int, Error: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose Result.Error(100): when e: Error: e else 0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 100 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tests_45_VARIANT_45_IS}{\*\bkmkend ID_tests_45_VARIANT_45_IS}\b\fs28\f1 7.12.2 Variant Is Expression\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The is expression tests if a union value matches a variant.\sa330\par\trowd\trql\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Input\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \b\fs22\f1 Result\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union: (Ok: int, Error: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if Result.Ok(50) is Ok : 1 else 0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 1 \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trql \clvertalt\cellx4320\clvertalt\cellx8640 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl257 \fs19\f2 define Result union: (Ok: int, Error: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if Result.Error(33) is Ok : 1 else 0\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl286 \fs22\f2 0 \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_modules}{\*\bkmkend ID_modules}\b\fs35\f1 8 Modules and Packages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_overview}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_overview}\fs31 8.1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A Scaly program can be split across several source files, and it can depend on other packages. There are no build files, manifests, or configuration files \'97 three statements inside the source itself organize everything:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22\f2 module\f0  loads another source file into the current program or package.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22\f2 package\f0  declares a dependency on another package.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22\f2 use\f0  makes types defined in another module visible in the current file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_module}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_module}\b\fs31\f1 8.2 Modules\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Every source file is a module. The statement \f2 module shapes\f0  loads the file \f2 shapes.scaly\f0  from the directory of the file that declares it and makes it part of the program:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 ; shapes.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Box(width: int, height: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    function area(this) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        width * height\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; myprog.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
module shapes\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
use myprog.shapes.Box\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let b Box(3, 4)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if b.area() = 12\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("area is 12")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 A module may declare child modules of its own. Those live in a directory named after the parent module: if \f2 compiler.scaly\f0  declares \f2 module lexer\f0 , the child is loaded from \f2 compiler/lexer.scaly\f0 . This mirrors how the standard library and the compiler themselves are laid out.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Top-level statements of a loaded module are ignored \'97 only its definitions become part of the program. Programs have top-level statements; libraries consist of definitions only.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_package}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_package}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 8.3 Packages\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A package is a source distribution. Its version is encoded in its filesystem path \'97 \f2 packages/scaly/0.1.0/\f0  \'97 and that path is the single source of truth; there is no separate version manifest. The statement\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 package scaly 0.1.0\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 declares a dependency on that package. Packages are searched in \f2 ./packages/\f0  first, then \f2 /usr/share/scaly/packages/\f0 , then any paths given with \f2 -I\f0 . When SCALY_HOME is set, the standard packages are resolved under \f2 $SCALY_HOME/packages/\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Multiple versions of the same package can coexist in one program: the version is baked into the mangled symbol names, so they never collide. Dependencies are not transitively visible \'97 a package sees only the packages it declares itself \'97 and circular package dependencies are forbidden.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 The standard library package \f2 scaly\f0  is the implicit \i prelude\i0 : every program depends on it automatically, and its types are visible everywhere without any \f2 use\f0  statement. Pass \f2 --no-prelude\f0  to compile without it, for bare-metal or embedded targets. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib}{\fldrslt Appendix A}} for what the standard library provides.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_use}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_use}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 8.4 Imports and Visibility\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Type definitions are private to their module by default. A type defined in another module \'97 even one loaded into the same program with \f2 module\f0  \'97 is hidden until the current file imports it with a \f2 use\f0  statement. This keeps every file's dependencies explicit: the list of \f2 use\f0  statements at the top of a file is a complete inventory of the outside types the file relies on.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_use_45_default}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_use_45_default}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.4.1 What Is Visible Without an Import\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Three kinds of types need no \f2 use\f0  statement:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Types defined \i in the same file\i0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Types of the \i prelude\i0  \'97 the standard library (\f2 String\f0 , \f2 Vector\f0 , \f2 Option\f0 , \'85) is always visible.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 The namespace of a \i declared child module\i0 . When a file declares \f2 module geometry\f0  and \f2 geometry.scaly\f0  defines a namespace of the same name, that namespace is visible in the declaring file, so its members can be reached qualified:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl257 \fs19\f2 ; geometry.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define geometry\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    function unit_area() returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; myprog.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
module geometry\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let a geometry.unit_area()   ; qualified access, no use needed\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Visibility applies to named type definitions. Free functions are resolved through their namespaces and are not gated separately.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_use_45_explicit}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_use_45_explicit}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.4.2 Importing a Single Type\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A \f2 use\f0  statement names a type by its full dotted path. The path starts with the program or package name, followed by the module path, ending in the type name:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 use myprog.shapes.Box            ; Box from module shapes of program myprog\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
use scalyc.compiler.Modeler      ; Modeler from package scalyc\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 A module that is named after the single type it defines \'97 the file \f2 Modeler.scaly\f0  defining \f2 Modeler\f0  \'97 is addressed once, as the path spells it; the module and the type do not appear as two separate path segments.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_mod_45_use_45_glob}{\*\bkmkend ID_mod_45_use_45_glob}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.4.3 Importing Everything from a Module\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A trailing \f2 *\f0  imports every type a module defines:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 module shapes\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
use myprog.shapes.*\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let b Box(3, 4)              ; Box is visible through the glob\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
