365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_stdlib_45_function_45_minus}{\*\bkmkend ID_stdlib_45_function_45_minus}\fs28 A.3.1 \f2 function -(right: int) returns int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Unary negation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \i\lang1024\f2 right\i0\f0 : int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value to negate\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_stdlib_45_function_45_plus}{\*\bkmkend ID_stdlib_45_function_45_plus}\b\fs28\f1 A.3.2 \f2 function +(right: int) returns int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Unary plus (identity).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \i\lang1024\f2 right\i0\f0 : int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value to return unchanged\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_stdlib_45_function_45_abs}{\*\bkmkend ID_stdlib_45_function_45_abs}\b\fs28\f1 A.3.3 \f2 function abs(x: int) returns int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Absolute value.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \i\lang1024\f2 x\i0\f0 : int\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value whose absolute value to compute\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}{\*\bkmkend ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}\b\fs35\f1 B. Bootstrap History and the Seed\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb179\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly is self-hosted, and the committed \i seed\i0  \'97 the compiler emitted as its own textual LLVM IR under \f2 seed/\f0  \'97 is the sole bootstrap root. The normal way to build the compiler is from that seed, as described in {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_tut_45_build}{\fldrslt Section 2.1.2}}; it needs only LLVM 18 and a C compiler. A single seed covers every 64-bit little-endian (LP64) target, and a CI matrix builds and verifies it natively on macOS and Linux, on both x86-64 and AArch64.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 The seed is refreshed by \f2 tools/seed.sh\f0 : it bootstraps a compiler from the committed seed, re-emits the compiler sources with it, and verifies the \i fixed point\i0  \'97 the seed must reproduce itself byte-identically \'97 before \f2 tools/install-seed.sh\f0  installs the new files. Textual IR is diffable and auditable, which is why Scaly ships it instead of opaque per-platform binaries. See \f2 RELEASING.md\f0  in the repository for the full seed-distribution checklist.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Historically, the self-hosted compiler was bootstrapped from a reference implementation written in C++ (which had itself replaced an earlier TypeScript bootstrapper). That C++ stage-0 compiler was retired in July 2026, once the seed became self-sufficient: its sources are frozen under \f2 retired/scalyc0/\f0  as a historical reference, it has no build, and it cannot parse the current language. No C++ toolchain appears anywhere in today's build.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Targets with a different data model (32-bit, big-endian, or LLP64 such as Windows) are not covered by the seed; supporting one would mean generalizing the compiler's size and alignment emission off its fixed 64-bit constants and minting a seed for that data model.\hyphpar0\par}
